    <h2>The Real Problem Is the Red Clay</h2>

    <p>Morgan County soil is expansive clay — it swells when it takes on water and shrinks back when it dries, and it does this every wet season and every drought. A concrete slab is rigid; the ground under it is not. Pour a driveway directly onto that native clay and the slab has to ride every swell and shrink cycle beneath it, and rigid things that get bent enough eventually crack. This is why the same driveway design that lasts thirty years in sandy soil fails in five here. The homeowner sees a cracked slab and thinks they got bad concrete. They got bad ground preparation.</p>

    <h2>Why the Common Fix Fails</h2>

    <p>The common response is to pour thicker or add more rebar, and neither addresses the actual cause. A thicker slab on unstable clay is just a heavier slab that still moves with the ground. Here is what goes wrong on the cheap jobs: the crew scrapes off the grass, forms the edges, and pours straight onto the clay to save the cost and the day of labor that a proper base takes. Rebar does not stop a slab from cracking — it holds the pieces together and level after it does, which is useful, but it is not prevention. Prevention happens under the concrete, before a single yard is poured.</p>

    <h2>The Counterintuitive Truth About Cracks</h2>

    <p>Here is the honest admission every good concrete finisher will make and every salesman will hide: all concrete cracks. There is no such thing as a crack-free slab over expansive clay, and anyone who promises you one is either lying or about to blame you when it happens. The entire craft is not preventing cracks — it is controlling where they happen. That is what a control joint is: a deliberately weakened line, cut to about a quarter of the slab's depth within the first 24 hours, that tells the concrete "if you must crack, crack here, straight and hidden, instead of wandering across the middle of the driveway." A slab with joints every 8 to 10 feet cracks invisibly. A slab with joints 20 feet apart, or none, cracks wherever it wants.</p>

    <h2>What Actually Works</h2>

    <p>A driveway built to last on Hartselle clay is a stack of correct decisions: strip the topsoil and organic material out, lay and compact 4 inches of crushed stone so the slab rides on stable base instead of living clay, pour a mix in the 3,000-to-4,000-psi range, finish it only after the bleed water has left the surface, cut the control joints on the right grid within a day, and cure it instead of letting it flash-dry in the sun. Skip the curing and you get the surface spalling that flakes off in Alabama's freeze-thaw winters. None of these steps show up in a photo of the finished driveway, which is exactly why they are the ones cut. This is the same base-prep discipline a good <a href="/locations/hartselle/general-contractor/">general contractor</a> insists on under an addition slab, and it is why concrete and site work travel together.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Concrete Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and where the honest money goes:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Standard broom-finish driveway or patio</strong> — about $6 to $9 per square foot installed.</li>
      <li><strong>Stamped or colored decorative concrete</strong> — roughly $12 to $18 per square foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Base prep</strong> — folded into the price, and the first thing a lowball bid quietly deletes; ask how many inches of compacted stone are included.</li>
      <li><strong>Tear-out and repour</strong> — priced higher because removing a failed slab and hauling it off is real labor before the new one starts.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A driveway of roughly 600 square feet done right lands near $4,200 to $6,000. A bid noticeably below that usually found its savings underground, where you cannot see it until the slab tells on it.</p>

    <h2>When to Act</h2>

    <p>Spring and fall pours cure best — you want moderate temperatures, not a July slab flashing off its surface water in an afternoon or a December pour fighting a freeze before it sets. If an existing slab has a single tight crack it can often be sealed and left alone; if it is heaving, spalling in sheets, or cracked into rocking pieces, that is a base failure that sealing will not fix, and it is worth pricing a proper tear-out and repour before it gets worse. Keeping the surface sealed and the joints filled buys real years either way.</p>

    <h2>Questions Hartselle Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Why is my new driveway cracking already?</strong></p>
    <p>The base, not the concrete. Poured on uncompacted red clay with joints spaced too far apart, a slab cracks where it wants. Compacted stone base and joints every 8 to 10 feet fix it.</p>

    <p><strong>Can you pour concrete that won't crack?</strong></p>
    <p>No, and be suspicious of anyone who says yes. Good work controls where it cracks — into hidden joints — instead of pretending to prevent it.</p>

    <p><strong>What does a driveway cost?</strong></p>
    <p>About $6 to $9 a square foot for standard finish; a 600-square-foot driveway runs roughly $4,200 to $6,000 with real base prep.</p>

